 ],
 "literal": "My lord, even eating poor gruel, is one of great shoulders, O mother! / We indeed, even remaining in the outer suburbs, are like golden beauty, O mother! / If my lord, opposing in battle, enters the battlefield, / in that place of the great town having festivals making a bustling sound, / to the warriors who are bewildered and perished, / he is like the lord of the ghat which the salt-merchants fear!",
 "translation": "My lord, even when eating poor gruel, has great shoulders, O mother! We, even while living in the outer suburbs, are like gold, O mother! If my lord, opposing in battle, enters the battlefield, to the warriors who wander in bewilderment in the festive areas of the bustling great town, he is like the lord of a dangerous river-crossing that makes even the salt-merchants tremble with fear!",
 "modern": "என் தலைவன் எளிய கஞ்சியை உணவாகக் குடித்தாலும் பெரிய தோள்களை உடையவன், தாயே! நாம் ஊருக்குப் புறம்பே வசித்தாலும் பொன்னைப் போன்றவர்கள், தாயே! என் தலைவன் போரை எதிர்த்துப் போர்க்களத்தில் புகுந்தால், ஆரவாரமுடைய பெரிய ஊரின் திருவிழா நடக்கும் இடங்களில் மயங்கித் திரியும் வீரர்களுக்கு, உப்பு வணிகர்கள் அஞ்சும் ஆற்றின் துறையைப் போன்றவன் ஆவான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by a young woman to her mother, expressing her deep love and admiration for the Chola king Porvaiko Perunarkilli. She describes how her lord, despite eating simple gruel, possesses magnificent, strong shoulders, while she and her companions remain beautiful like gold even in the outskirts of the town. When he enters the battlefield, he strikes terror into the hearts of opposing warriors, just like a treacherous river-crossing that terrifies salt-merchants who travel with heavy carts. The imagery of the salt-merchants fearing the dangerous ghat perfectly conveys the overwhelming power and formidable nature of the hero in battle. Key words include 'புற்கை' (poor gruel), 'மள்ளர்' (warriors), and 'துறையன்' (lord of the ghat).",
